{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 3, :comments ["<b>From wine and strong drink he shall abstain.</b> Wine is what is aged; shekhar is tirosh; others said that wine is the new wine and shekhar is the aged wine, for it intoxicates, as Onkelos translated: חמר חדת ועתיק — new wine and old. And he is commanded to keep away from five things that come forth from the vine: the wine, the grapes, the raisins, the ḥarutz (seeds) and the zag (skins). For each of these five he transgresses a negative prohibition, and the unripe grapes are included under 'grapes,' and vinegar is included under 'wine,' since at first it was wine and its prohibition was not lifted when it turned sour. And they said in tractate Nazir: if he ate fresh grapes and dried grapes, seeds and skins, and squeezed a cluster of grapes and drank — he receives five lashes." "<b>And by way of</b> the Kabbalah: 'When a man or woman clearly utges the vow of a nazirite' — I have already explained to you in the section Emor: the high priest is like whom? Behold, the nazir is above him, for he is above the attributes, cleaving to the very essence of the supreme ḥesed (mercy). And because he is crowned with the rank of nezirut he is called 'nazir' in the sense of nezer (crown), and this is 'the nezer of his Elohim is upon his head,' 'a razor shall not pass over his head,' 'he shall let the locks of the hair of his head grow long,' 'and he shall sanctify his head.' And this is 'when he clearly utges' — from the language of פלאות חכמה (wonders of wisdom), and 'to vow a vow' is the place of binah (understanding). And he is warned about three things: about wine, about shaving, and about corpse-impurity. And the verse made in this three divisions, mentioning in them three times 'all the days of': first division — 'all the days of his naziriteship, from all that...' etc.; second division — 'all the days of his naziriteship a razor shall not pass over his head'; third division — 'all the days that he separates himself to YHWH he shall not come upon a dead soul.' He was warned about wine and corpse-impurity in order to distance himself from the left side. He was warned about shaving because a person's hair is his strength, and so regarding Samson (Judges 16:17): 'if I am shaven my strength departs from me.' And they have no end, and they grow all the days of a person's life, even after his death if he stands in a moist place. And all this alludes to the branches and plantings and potencies of the Name, which are fine and inner havyot (emanations), fashioned like the threads of flames spreading to every side, having no end or limit, no terminus. Therefore he was commanded to grow them — as it says, 'he shall let the locks of the hair of his head grow long' — and was warned not to cut them, for 'the nezer of his Elohim is upon his head.' And if he cuts the hair of his head, which is a model of the plantings, it is as though he severs and separates the Name from its branches." "<b>And behold,</b> Samson — when the hair of his head was cut, it was accounted to him as a severing, and immediately his strength departed from him. And that is the strength of the Name, and this is the sense of ותאלצהו — that he would return to the attribute of judgment; they read in it ותאצלהו (and it diminished him), that is, the withholding of the emanation (atzilut), for the spirit of prophecy departed from him because the power of the Name had departed from him on account of his having cut. And the Torah commanded the nazir, when he shaves his head, that he give his hair onto the fire that is under the sacrifice of the well-being offerings, because they are holy and a model of holiness. And the verse was stricter with him than with the high priest, who is not to defile himself for his father or his mother — and everyone understood that the nazir in his hair is a model. And our Rabbis of blessed memory said: five were created in the likeness of the model above, and they were stricken: Absalom in his hair, Saul in his neck, Samson in his strength, Asa in his feet, Zedekiah in his eyes. And concerning these havyot Solomon said (Song of Songs 5:11): 'his locks are curling,' and likewise Daniel (Daniel 7:9): 'and the hair of his head like pure wool,' and this is clear." "<b>And shekhar</b> — which the verse mentions — refers to anything from which a person may become intoxicated, [and it is the wine and strong drink mentioned regarding Aaron and his sons when they come to the Tent of Meeting (Leviticus 10:9)]. And the word חרצנים has no parallel in all of Scripture, and likewise the word זג. Now, חרצן is the seed and זג is the skin that is on the outside. And the sage Rabbi Abraham interpreted the reverse: חרצן is the outer one and זג is the inner one."], :book :numbers, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 6, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:30:07.448043081Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}